VACUUM filesystem hole punching
==============================

Lazy VACUUM can deallocate the filesystem blocks backing completely
empty heap pages ("hole punching"), reclaiming disk space without
changing the logical size of the relation.  The feature is opt-in via
the vacuum_punch_hole GUC (boolean, default off, PGC_USERSET, so it can
be set per-session and applies to autovacuum workers as well).  Like
vacuum_truncate, it follows the triple interface: GUC (global default),
reloption vacuum_punch_hole (per-table, ternary), and VACUUM (PUNCH_HOLE
...) option (per-command).  Resolution: explicit option > reloption >
GUC > off.  When disabled, the punch path exits immediately with no
bookkeeping overhead.

A second knob, vacuum_punch_hole_min_size (integer kB, default 0=auto),
sets the minimum hole size: ranges smaller than this are not punched.
Unlike vacuum_punch_hole, this is GUC-only (no reloption or VACUUM option):
per-segment fstatvfs() auto-detection already handles tablespace
heterogeneity, and no credible per-table/per-command use case exists.  The
auto default computes per segment via fstatvfs(): max(BLCKSZ, filesystem
block size).  This handles tablespaces on different filesystems correctly,
and avoids useless syscalls on filesystems with large allocation units.

This complements vacuum_truncate rather than replacing it: truncation
only reclaims empty pages at the tail of the relation and requires an
AccessExclusive lock, while punching reclaims empty pages anywhere in
the relation and needs no lock beyond what VACUUM already holds.  All
deallocation is best-effort: on platforms or filesystems without hole
punching support it silently does nothing.


Lifecycle of a punched page
---------------------------

Candidate selection happens in lazy_scan_new_or_empty(), during the
heap scan.  A page qualifies when it is PageIsNew() (all zeroes) or, with
the GUC on, PageIsLogicallyEmpty(): it has no live line pointers.
"Logically empty" is broader than PageIsEmpty(): a page whose tuples
were all pruned away still carries its (now unused) line pointer array.

Only LP_UNUSED line pointers count as empty.  LP_DEAD items block
punching even though they hold no live data: index entries may still
reference those TIDs until VACUUM's index cleanup deletes them, which
happens after the heap scan that selects punch candidates.  Punching
such a page would let the file range read back as zeroes (a new page)
if the buffer were evicted, losing the LP_DEAD markers that tell index
scans to skip those TIDs; a concurrently inserted tuple could then
reuse the same offsets while stale index TIDs still point at them.

Each candidate must also pass lazy_punch_page_safe():

* the page is not marked all-visible (see "Visibility map" below), and
* the page's LSN predates the redo pointer of the last completed
  checkpoint (see "WAL and crash recovery" below).

Pages failing the gate fall back to the stock behavior of being marked
all-visible/all-frozen, so future vacuums can still skip them.

Candidates arrive in ascending block order, so they are accumulated
into contiguous runs (at most PUNCH_BATCH_MAX_BLOCKS = 128 blocks per
batch, keeping re-verified pages hot under VACUUM's 256-page buffer
access strategy ring).  A run is flushed when it breaks and once more
after the heap scan, before lazy_truncate_heap() (which may remove
trailing pages outright).

The flush cannot punch immediately: the scan's lock on the page is
released on return, and a page seen as empty might gain tuples before
the flush (it was just advertised in the FSM as free space).  The flush
therefore re-verifies every page under a freshly acquired exclusive
buffer lock, then punches each maximal contiguous range of still-empty
pages with one mdpunchhole() call per relation segment.

After punching, each page's cached buffer image is zeroed with MemSet()
but deliberately NOT marked dirty: the on-disk image is already zeroes
where the punch worked, or still the old empty page where the
filesystem doesn't support punching -- both read as an empty page.  This
keeps the cache coherent with the disk and makes the buffers PageIsNew,
so the next insert into the page takes the XLOG_HEAP_INIT_PAGE path
(see "WAL and crash recovery").

ReadBufferExtended() and mdpunchhole() can raise ERROR (I/O errors,
failure to open a segment) while buffer content locks are held, which
abort processing would otherwise leak.  The flush wraps its work in
PG_TRY: buffers are zeroed even on the error path, so no page is ever
left punched on disk but non-zero in cache, and every held lock and pin
is released before rethrowing.


Locking
-------

No relation-level lock beyond what VACUUM already holds: the file size
never changes and only provably-empty pages are touched.

Per page, the flush takes an exclusive buffer content lock and holds it
across the punch.  The page is re-verified empty under that lock, so no
concurrent backend can insert into it in the meantime (any inserter
needs the same lock).  Locks are acquired conditionally and strictly in
ascending block order, so the flush never blocks and cannot deadlock;
pages that can't be locked, that gained content since the scan, or that
no longer pass the safety gate are simply left for a future vacuum.

Pages emptied by this vacuum's own pruning are deliberately NOT
punched: the second heap pass dirties them and bumps their LSN, so
punching would be both useless (a later writeback would reallocate the
blocks) and unsafe (recovery could replay WAL onto the punched page).
They become punchable via the empty-page path in a later vacuum, once a
checkpoint has passed.  They are also not marked all-visible, so that
later vacuum will actually visit them.


WAL and crash recovery
----------------------

Punching is a filesystem metadata operation and is not WAL-logged.
Crash safety rests on two properties instead:

1. The LSN gate.  A page is only punched when its LSN predates the redo
   pointer of the last completed checkpoint.  Every WAL record for the
   page is then older than the point crash recovery would start from,
   so none of them can ever be replayed onto the punched (all-zero)
   page.  The redo pointer is read from pg_control under ControlFileLock
   -- GetRedoRecPtr() is not suitable, as it returns the redo pointer
   published when the current checkpoint started, and a page changed
   between the two could be punched and then have that record replayed
   if the checkpoint never completes.  The pointer is read once per
   VACUUM and cached; a checkpoint completing afterwards only moves the
   true redo pointer forward, so the cached value stays conservative.
   If pg_control can't be read, punching is skipped (best effort).

2. Page re-initialization on reuse.  After the punch the buffer is clean
   zeroes, i.e. PageIsNew.  The next insert therefore emits
   XLOG_HEAP_INIT_PAGE, a full page image.  Crash recovery can always
   re-initialize a punched page -- even with full_page_writes=off,
   because every WAL record written before the punch predates the last
   completed checkpoint and is never replayed, while the first record
   written after the punch carries a page-init.  If the buffer is
   evicted before reuse, the page reads back as zeroes from disk, which
   every code path already handles as a new page (PageInit on first
   use).

Unlogged and temporary relations use the same LSN gate.  This is
conservative -- they are truncated on crash recovery, so punching their
empty pages would be safe regardless of LSN -- but the uniform check is
simpler and never wrong.

The crash-safety argument is exercised by
src/test/modules/test_misc/t/018_vacuum_punch_hole_crash.pl: punch,
insert into punched space, kill -9 before the next checkpoint, verify
data integrity after restart with full_page_writes both on and off.


Visibility map
--------------

Punched pages are deliberately NOT marked all-visible before punching.
The punch zeroes the page header, dropping its PD_ALL_VISIBLE flag,
while the visibility map would still claim all-visible.  heap_insert()
clears the visibility map only when the page header has PD_ALL_VISIBLE
set, so a stale VM bit would survive reuse of the page and corrupt
index-only scans.  Pages that are already all-visible are left alone
(punching them would require clearing the VM first); they are rare,
because vacuums using this code punch empty pages instead of marking
them all-visible.  A side effect is that punched pages are revisited by
future vacuums rather than skipped via the VM -- which is required
anyway for a promoted standby to discover the free space (see
"Replication").


Buffer manager and checkpointer
-------------------------------

Zeroing the buffers without dirtying them keeps the cache coherent:
both possible on-disk states (zeroes where the punch worked, the old
empty page where the filesystem lacks support) read as an empty page.
Clean buffers are never written by the checkpointer, so there is
nothing to fsync and no checkpoint interaction.  Cost-based vacuum
delay (vacuum_delay_point) is applied before the punch I/O.

The flush acquires up to 128 buffer locks in exclusive mode (one per
page in the batch) and holds them across the filesystem syscall.  This
is correctness-critical: the re-verification under lock is what prevents
a concurrent backend from inserting into a page after we've decided to
punch it.  The downside is that a checkpointer (or any other backend)
trying to acquire one of those buffers will stall until the batch
completes.  In practice the syscall is fast (a single fallocate per
segment), so stalls are brief, but they are possible.

Replication
-----------

Physical replication ships WAL, not filesystem blocks, and punching is
not WAL-logged.  Standbys therefore keep the blocks allocated: data is
identical, only physical space usage diverges.  This is safe because the
file size is unchanged (all platform calls use KEEP_SIZE semantics), so
relation size metadata stays consistent, and because a punched page is
indistinguishable from a new page, which the standby's replay already
handles.  On promotion the standby works normally.  Base backups copy
the files as-is; hole ranges are either preserved as sparse or expanded
by the copy method, both correct.  Logical replication is unaffected
(row changes only).


Interaction with other subsystems
---------------------------------

* FSM: punched pages are recorded with their free space as usual, so
  they are reused normally; a later write transparently reallocates the
  filesystem blocks.
* Index cleanup ordering: the punch flush runs after lazy_scan_heap()
  (which includes index vacuum/cleanup and the second heap pass that
  converts LP_DEAD to LP_UNUSED) and before lazy_truncate_heap().  A
  page that was LP_DEAD-only during the scan is not a candidate this
  pass; after the second pass makes it LP_UNUSED-only, a later
  post-checkpoint vacuum can punch it.
* pg_visibility / pageinspect: a punched page reads as a new (empty)
  page, as expected.
* Parallel VACUUM: candidate selection and the punch flush run in the
  leader's heap-scan phase; index workers are unaffected.
* VACUUM VERBOSE reports the number of pages whose filesystem blocks
  were deallocated.


smgr layer: mdpunchhole()
-------------------------

mdpunchhole() (src/backend/storage/smgr/md.c) deallocates the storage
for a range of blocks known to read back as zeroes, without changing
the file size; a later write transparently reallocates.  The range may
span relation segments and is split so each segment gets a single call
-- one syscall per segment instead of one per block.  It never creates
missing segment files, retries on EINTR, and uses the segment's raw fd
(via FileGetRawDesc()) since the segment is already open.

Platform backends (selected by feature macros, so a platform gaining an
API is picked up automatically):

* Linux: fallocate(FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE | FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE)
* macOS: fcntl(F_PUNCHHOLE)
* FreeBSD 14+: fspacectl(SPACECTL_DEALLOC).  Note: on filesystems without
  a native deallocate implementation (e.g. UFS, which uses the default
  vop_stddeallocate), the "punch" performs real zero-fill writes -- wasted
  I/O, the opposite of the feature's goal -- yet success is still counted
  in punched_blocks.  The range reads back as zeroes either way, so this
  is only a reporting inaccuracy, but VERBOSE output should not be read
  as "space reclaimed" on such filesystems.
* Solaris/illumos: fcntl(F_FREESP) (true hole punch on ZFS; UFS/tmpfs
  only implement the truncate form and return EINVAL, handled below)
* Others (NetBSD, OpenBSD, DragonFly, Windows): silent no-op

This is strictly best-effort and never raises: EOPNOTSUPP and ENOSYS
mark the segment remembered as unsupported (mdfd_punchhole_unsupported),
so an unsupported filesystem costs one failed syscall per segment file.
EINVAL is treated as unsupported only on macOS and Solaris/illumos (where
the platform documentation describes it as a legitimate rejection, not a
bug); on Linux and FreeBSD, EINVAL indicates a bug in our range
construction and is logged at LOG.  Any other unexpected errno is also
logged at LOG.  The caller must guarantee every block in the range is
empty and cannot concurrently gain content.

Known external risk: this relies on the kernel implementing hole
punching correctly for the given range.  A 2026 Linux series fixing data
loss in truncate_inode_partial_folio() -- the generic page-cache path
shared by hole punching -- was still unmerged at the time of writing;
that is a kernel bug affecting all FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE users, not
something this code can work around.


When it helps (and when it doesn't)
------------------------------------

Punching deallocates whole filesystem blocks, so it only reclaims space
when the storage stack's allocation unit is no larger than the database
page size (block_size, normally 8 kB): if the filesystem block size or
the disk sector size is larger than a database page, punching a page's
range frees nothing.  On SSDs the same holds for the device's delete
(deallocate) granularity, and the freed blocks only reach the device if
the filesystem issues discards for them.  On such storage, enabling
vacuum_punch_hole gains nothing while still costing a syscall per
relation segment.

It helps workloads that leave many fully-empty interior pages which
truncation cannot reach -- e.g. bulk deletes scattered through a table
-- on filesystems with working hole punching (ext4, XFS, btrfs, APFS,
ZFS, tmpfs where supported).  pg_relation_size() is unchanged by
punching (it reports the logical file size); physical usage is visible
via the file's st_blocks, which t/016_vacuum_punch_hole.pl checks.


